--- body ---
## Context

Author, 2026-09-06: "Accounting must add up to the correct totals." Two
independent reviewers (correctness and red-team) found real gaps in
`bench/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js`'s failure counting while reviewing PR #369
(ticket 0691's "N documents could not be indexed" summary line), which
surfaces this existing counting logic into a first-class, trusted UI
element for the first time. The counting logic itself predates 0691 and
is in scope here, not there.

## The two gaps

1. **Over-report.** `scheduler.js`'s failure branch is
   `if (!ok || after.status !== 'current') throw ...` -- a native
   `ensure()` that returns `true` but whose post-hoc re-`inspect()` does
   not show `status === 'current'` increments `state.failed`. That is a
   *postcondition-verification mismatch*, not necessarily proof the
   document was never extracted. Confirmed reachable by the existing test
   `native success without current persisted cache counts as failure`.
2. **Under-report.** `inspect()` throwing during census maps to
   `status: 'inspection-error'` (scheduler.js census loop), which sits
   outside the four-status whitelist that populates `candidates`. A
   document whose attachment is genuinely broken/inaccessible at
   inspection time never becomes a candidate, never reaches `ensure()`,
   and never touches `state.failed` -- it is invisible in the new banner,
   surfacing only as a `counts['inspection-error']` entry inside the
   collapsed diagnostics block. The banner can read "0 documents n'ont
   pas pu etre indexes" while real inspection failures exist unreported.

These pull in opposite directions and must both be fixed together --
fixing only one without the other trades one wrong total for another.

## What "adds up" means here

Every document that is not currently, verifiably indexed must be
accounted for in exactly one bucket, and the buckets' total must equal
the library's non-indexed count. Concretely:
- `inspection-error` documents must be counted as not-indexed (join the
  failure tally, or an explicit adjacent category -- pick one and make
  the UI honest about which), not silently dropped from every count.
- The "native success but not persisted" case should stay counted as a
  failure for accounting purposes (the document genuinely has no current
  index, regardless of subtlety in cause) -- the fix is not to stop
  counting it, but to make sure the *other* gap (inspection-error) is
  counted too, so the total is complete rather than selectively wrong.
- If the sitter cannot distinguish some failure subtypes cleanly, the UI
  may show one honest combined total rather than a false precision split
  -- do not invent a distinction the code cannot actually verify.

## Work

- Read the current census/candidate logic in `scheduler.js` (the
  four-status whitelist and the `inspection-error` status path) before
  changing anything.
- Include `inspection-error` in whatever total feeds the user-facing
  failure count, without breaking the existing `failed-session`
  suppression-by-identity behavior for genuine extraction failures.
- Add a test that independently sums every `counts[*]` bucket against
  the library's total **attachment** count -- the count the census
  actually enumerates (`SELECT itemID FROM itemAttachments`), never a
  count of distinct Zotero items. Do not assume one Zotero entry has
  exactly one PDF: a reference can carry zero, one, or several
  attachments the sitter processes independently (SPEC.md D6 twin
  attachments), and a standalone attachment with no parent item is also
  valid. Reconciling against `Zotero.Items.getAll().length` instead of
  the candidate/queue length would silently pass on a single-attachment
  fixture and be wrong on any real library where an item has 0 or more
  than 1 attachment. This is the actual "add up to the correct totals"
  property, not per-scenario spot checks.
- Do not change what counts as "indexed" (`status === 'current'`) --
  only fix what happens to documents that are not.

## Acceptance

- [ ] A document that throws during `inspect()` is reflected in the
      user-facing failure total, not just in collapsed diagnostics.
- [ ] A reconciliation test: sum of all `counts[*]` buckets equals the
      total candidate count (attachment rows the census enumerates, not
      distinct Zotero items), for a fixture exercising all branches
      (current, missing-pack, stale-source, stale-processor,
      invalid-pack, inspection-error, failed-session) AND including at
      least one item with two supported attachments and one item with
      zero -- proving the reconciliation is not silently assuming a 1:1
      item-to-attachment mapping.
- [ ] No existing passing test regresses.
- [ ] `unsupported-pack`/`missing-source` documents are reflected honestly
      in the coverage total, not silently excluded from both numerator
      and denominator in a way that makes 100% unreachable.
- [ ] A persisted, verified pack (`host.observed()` returns true) is
      never counted as a failure, even if `observed()` itself throws on
      a later check.
